@page "/admin/welcome"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.Authorize]
@inject ManagementGateway Gateway
@inject DataGateway Caller

@*
    The first run.

    It does NOT create an account, and that is the whole shape of it: the bootstrap administrator
    exists before this screen can be reached at all — the deployment supplied an address and a
    password file, and without them nobody signs in. So the first step is already done by the time
    anybody reads this, and what remains is the three things a new project actually needs.

    Each step reports its own state rather than being ticked off a stored checklist. A checklist
    that remembered would be a second source of truth about the project, and would be wrong the
    first time somebody changed something through the API instead.
*@
<PageTitle>First run · Alvo</PageTitle>

<div class="a-stack" style="max-width:820px">
    <PageHeader Title="Welcome to Alvo"
                Subtitle="One descriptor drives everything here: entities, the rules that guard them, and what runs on a write. These are the three things a new project needs, in the order they make sense." />

    <Step Done="true" Title="You are signed in as the administrator this host was configured with">
        <code class="a-mono">@_email</code> — seeded from
        <code class="a-mono">Alvo:Admin:BootstrapEmail</code> and the password file beside it. This
        account holds full management access independently of the descriptor's
        <code class="a-mono">access</code> block, which is what makes a project whose rules admit
        nobody recoverable.
    </Step>

    <Step Done="@(_entities > 0)" Title="@($"Define what the backend is — {_entities} entit{(_entities == 1 ? "y" : "ies")} so far")">
        An entity is a table Alvo creates and keeps in step with the descriptor. Add one in Schema,
        give it fields, and preview what the change does to the database before it runs.
        <div style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
            <a class="a-btn a-btn--sm" href="@($"{AlvoAdmin.BasePath}/schema")">Open Schema</a>
            <a class="a-btn a-btn--ghost a-btn--sm" href="@($"{AlvoAdmin.BasePath}/schema/transfer")">Import a descriptor</a>
        </div>
    </Step>

    <Step Done="@_hasRules" Title="Say who may read and write each one">
        Nothing is reachable without an explicit rule — an entity with none refuses every caller,
        which is default-deny working rather than a gap. The Rules screen shows the predicate the
        engine compiled and answers <em>what would this caller get</em> with the engine's own verdict.
        <div style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
            <a class="a-btn a-btn--sm" href="@($"{AlvoAdmin.BasePath}/rules")">Open Rules</a>
        </div>
    </Step>

    <Step Done="@(_tenant is not null)" Title="@(_tenant is null ? "You hold no tenant, and tenant-scoped entities are closed to you" : "You act in one tenant")">
        @if (_tenant is null)
        {
            <text>
                An entity marked <code class="a-mono">tenancy: scoped</code> refuses a caller with no
                tenant — with a 403, before any rule is consulted. <strong>You cannot grant one to
                yourself</strong>: that is a deliberate refusal, because widening your own reach
                should go through the route that records who did it. Either create a second
                administrator in Access and have them grant you one, or keep this project's entities
                global.
            </text>
            <div style="margin-top:var(--space-3)">
                <a class="a-btn a-btn--sm" href="@($"{AlvoAdmin.BasePath}/access")">Open Access</a>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>
                Tenant <code class="a-mono">@_tenant.Value.Value</code>. Scoped entities show you the
                rows of this tenant and no other — and a row can never move between tenants.
            </text>
        }
    </Step>

    <div class="a-panel" style="padding:var(--space-5)">
        <span class="a-section-title">Everything here is a file</span>
        <p class="a-section-sub">
            The descriptor is the whole configuration, and it exports byte for byte. Commit it, apply
            it somewhere else, and you have the same backend — which is why nothing in this dashboard
            can do something the descriptor cannot say.
        </p>
        <a class="a-btn a-btn--sm" href="@($"{AlvoAdmin.BasePath}/schema/transfer")">Export the descriptor</a>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private TenantId? _tenant;
    private string _email = string.Empty;
    private int _entities;
    private bool _hasRules;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var context = await Caller.ContextAsync(CancellationToken.None);
        _tenant = context.Tenant;

        try
        {
            var schema = await Gateway.SchemaAsync(CancellationToken.None);
            _entities = schema.Entities.Count;

            var descriptor = await Gateway.DescriptorAsync(CancellationToken.None);
            _hasRules = schema.Entities.Any(
                entity => DescriptorLens.Rules(descriptor.DescriptorJson, entity.Name).Count > 0);

            var people = Gateway.CanAdministerPeople
                ? await Gateway.PeopleAsync(new AlvoUserQuery(), CancellationToken.None)
                : null;

            _email = people?.Users
                .FirstOrDefault(person => person.Id == context.User)?.Email
                ?? context.User.ToString();
        }
        catch (Exception exception) when (exception is ManagementForbiddenException
            or ManagementProjectNotFoundException or ManagementRequestException)
        {
            _email = context.User.ToString();
        }
    }
}
